---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getEntry, render } from 'astro:content';

const entry = await getEntry('docs', 'solver-comparison');
if (!entry) throw new Error('docs/solver-comparison.md not found in the repo');
const { Content } = await render(entry);
---

<Base
  title="Solver bake-off"
  description="Five solver families race on the same Opus Magnum instances: benchmark tables, optimality proofs, tractability cliffs."
>
  <div class="eyebrow">The centerpiece</div>
  <h1>Solver bake-off: can computers solve Opus Magnum puzzles?</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The full comparison document, rendered from
    <code>docs/solver-comparison.md</code>. Canonical optima
    <strong>3 / 11 / 12</strong> on the three shared puzzles, proved independently by
    three different arms. Click any table header to sort.
  </p>

  <h2>Optimality-proof ranking</h2>
  <p class="section-note">
    Finding a plan is cheap for everyone; <em>proving</em> no shorter plan exists is
    where the arms separate (§4 of the document below).
  </p>
  <ol class="ranking">
    <li>
      <span class="who">SAT (PySAT / CaDiCaL / kissat)</span> — proves all three
      canonical optima in ≤9.6&nbsp;s; UNSAT certificates 10–14× faster than clingo on
      the same questions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="who">Picat (planner, iterative deepening)</span> — proves all three
      in ≤62.6&nbsp;s; proof is structural — <code>best_plan</code> has no
      incumbent-at-timeout mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="who">MiniZinc / OR-Tools CP-SAT (multi-core)</span> — proves all
      three in ≤124.4&nbsp;s, and is the only MiniZinc engine that survives the
      canonical instances.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="who">clingo & Z3</span> — find the same optimal-length plans (clingo
      in ~1&nbsp;s) but prove only <code>single_transport</code>, even at 300&nbsp;s
      budgets.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <hr />

  <article class="prose wide">
    <Content />
  </article>
</Base>
